Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||12-21-1999||03:11 PM||||when starting or shutting off my car, (i think its lowwww rpm related) my... exhaust, i think, is rattling. anyone had this happen? i don't know what it is thats rattling..||||reg||1
Z||000001||mshill||12-21-1999||03:21 PM||||Check to see if one of the many support springs that hold the exhaust system in place has come loose.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||12-21-1999||03:22 PM||||Is it a V6?  If so there are many springs, clips and stuff that hold the stock muffler, check to see if any are missing, also does the cat. have a heat sheild? some times the spot welds come apart.  <P>Just a guess???||||reg||11
Z||000003||stsmithgt||12-21-1999||03:26 PM||||A rattle? only one? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Could be a number of things.  One that comes to mind is the heat shields on the engine.  One or more could be loose.  They're pretty loud if loose.  OR the guts to a catyletic converter can rattle like a mother.  How about a loose air cleaner lid?  Those are a few off the top of my head <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (I think there's something rattling in my head too.)||||reg||10
Z||000004||dogtired||12-21-1999||04:50 PM||||My vote would be the CAT.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Does it sound like rocks banging around in a hub cap? My 6 automatic sounded awful till you pulled it into gear, then I guess I just didn't notice. Anyway took CAT off Gutted it and reinstalled, now it runs and sounds good. Your muffler can do the same, but it usually just falls apart. The cat will come apart on the inside, and can plug your exhaust makeing the car run POOR. GOOD LUCK   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000005||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||06:52 PM||||I second the vote for heat shields.  Since they are VERY thin steel, they don't need much force to move and they resonate at a low frequency.  They often will make a one or two cycle rattle when the car is shutting down.  Many are welded on, others are attached with bolts... My suggestion... get the thing in the air and get under it and have someone else turn it off (or build yourself a remote switch).  After a few shutdowns you should be able to track it down enough.  Worse comes to worse and you don't have any bolts that you can tighten (like on some Cat's... heat shields) just bang on it w/ a BFH.  Since it is only a shield it cannot be damaged and the bend is usually enough to increase the resonance frequency into the non-audible range.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000006||Monkeyman||12-22-1999||10:43 PM||||William, would a "BFH" be something like a large, sexual hammer? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||JetroGT||12-23-1999||01:46 AM||||Don't worry. Call the CarX man.<P>Check the heat shield on the Y-pipe. When I had the heads replaced they forgot to bolt it back on. Every time I accelerated it rattled. Or it could be your deck lid vents.||||reg||10
Z||000008||william_thorniii||12-23-1999||09:09 AM||||Monkey,<P>Most of the car guys that I know don't understand the term hammer.  Since they are not used much under the hood.  However the BFH is an indespensable tool.  One can never complete suspension work w/o the BFH.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000009||Formula||12-23-1999||04:34 PM||||the heat sheild on my muffler was missing a bbolt and it made a rattling noise.  I also gutted the cat and that makes it sound sweet||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroSTETZ||12-23-1999||06:20 PM||||my cat is off, and the muffler is a few months old... also, it sounds like something... "bigger" and "more" than a heat shield... if a mount comes off or wears out, could it cause the engine to bang? a piece of my catalytic converter DID come loose, and every one in a while i can hear a little piece tinkling around in the pipe, but i dont know what to do. i know that when the cat was real bad my car sounded like a weed wacker and wouldnt go about 3k rpm, now it does 55 in 2nd at about 6000 (is this normal, weak, etc.) this is a 4spd man. v-6||||reg||1
